            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.211,
            0.461,
            0.236
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5.4 Prime Factorisation"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.253,
            0.551,
            0.275
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Checking if two numbers are co-prime"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.281,
            0.575,
            0.302
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Teacher: Are 56 and 63 co-prime?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.304,
            0.834,
            0.348
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Anshu and Guna: If they have a common factor other than 1, then they are not co-prime. Let us check."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.35,
            0.836,
            0.438
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Anshu: I can write 56 = 14 × 4 and 63 = 21 × 3. So, 14 and 4 are factors, of 56. Further, 21 and 3 are factors of 63. So, there are no common factors. The numbers are co-prime."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.44,
            0.834,
            0.508
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Guna: Hold on. I can also write 56 = 7 × 8 and 63 = 9 × 7. We see that 7 is a factor of both numbers, so, they are not co-prime."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.143,
            0.516,
            0.634,
            0.539
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Clearly Guna is right, as 7 is a common factor."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.545,
            0.497,
            0.569
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "But where did Anshu go wrong?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.577,
            0.834,
            0.621
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Writing 56 = 14 × 4 tells us that 14 and 4 are both factors of 56, but it does not tell all the factors of 56. The same holds for the factors of 63."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.623,
            0.833,
            0.665
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "both numbers."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.143,
            0.668,
            0.573,
            0.689
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_10_equation_15.jpg",
          "content": "80 = 40 \\times 2 = 20 \\times 4 = 10 \\times 8 = 16 \\times 5 = ? ? ?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.144,
            0.692,
            0.392,
            0.711
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "63 = 9 × 7 = 3 × 21 = ???"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.715,
            0.835,
            0.85
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We have written ‘???’ to say that there may be more ways to factorise these numbers. But if we take any of the given factorisations, for example, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "80 = 16 \\times 5"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "6 3 = 9 \\times 7 ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , then there are no common factors. Can we conclude that 80 and 63 are co-prime? As Anshu’s mistake above shows, we cannot conclude that as there may be other ways to factorise the numbers."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.851,
            0.833,
            0.896
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What this means is that we need a more systematic approach to check if two numbers are co-prime."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.884,
            0.941,
            0.918,
            0.959
          ],
          "content": []
        }
